WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Glenn Gers, Screenwriter: Doing your work&nbsp;
and putting it in front of an audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.960
starting the work, finishing it and presenting&nbsp;
it is the most important education you can get.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:15.200
Film Courage: I’m wondering if being an&nbsp;
office temp for the 12 years that you did it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.840
and then doing the writing&nbsp;
on the side or in free time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.960
fueled you because if you'd had a&nbsp;
more comfortable job where maybe money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.280
wasn't an option and trying to book work maybe&nbsp;
you would have given up on the screenwriting?

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:34.880
Glenn: I don't think I just personally the way I'm&nbsp;
built I would have I frequently gave up on jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:43.840
rather than than have them interfere with my what&nbsp;
I thought was my career but I do think that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:51.440
I think that life can really challenge people&nbsp;
trying to be artists that the demands of real life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:59.920
it's hard it is hard to build a way to get&nbsp;
some time and to feel it's worth it when it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.880
an abnormal thing to do it's not it most people&nbsp;
aren't doing it and most people's lives don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:13.440
require it most people have perfectly fine lives&nbsp;
without writing a novel or a screenplay or doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.080
you know whatever it is that that an artist wants&nbsp;
to do and and I think it's important to recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:27.760
that you are doing something a little offbeat and&nbsp;
that the most important thing I think you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:35.200
is find a way to comfortably do it in the spaces&nbsp;
you have to do it in whatever they are there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.200
are some people who would rather work for six&nbsp;
months and then take two months off and have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.120
two months to really work and other people would&nbsp;
rather work an hour a day or every other weekend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.160
or whatever it is you have to you have to look at&nbsp;
yourself you have to see what's productive for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:59.920
there's no right or wrong the the novelist simone&nbsp;
as french mystery novelist thriller novelist he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.160
used to write in intensely short bursts he would&nbsp;
he would plan out a novel and then reportedly he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.840
had a separate house built in his backyard because&nbsp;
he could and he would go to this other house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.280
and he would not talk to his family they would&nbsp;
drop the meals at the front door and for like two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.920
weeks and he wrote short novels he would be like&nbsp;
don't bother me I'm writing and he would go in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.680
then he would come out with a novel a couple weeks&nbsp;
later and then he would live his life and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:36.240
next time around he would go back in that's fine&nbsp;
there's other people who you know just like me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.600
office temping a half hour here half an hour there&nbsp;
at other people's desks or like I happen to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:48.320
morning person so I would get up extra early and&nbsp;
work in the morning before work but don't discount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:56.880
the amount of pressure that real life puts on&nbsp;
your art and I would say the thing to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:04.960
adapt your art to fit that life you know if&nbsp;
you like throwing all these references around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.800
the painter Matisse when he got older he&nbsp;
couldn't stand at his easel and paint anymore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:18.320
and he ended up doing these cut outs he would take&nbsp;
paper and cut out figures and they became gorgeous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.480
artwork called the cutouts there's a book called&nbsp;
jazz and a lot of other work he stopped painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.640
he couldn't physically paint anymore he shifted&nbsp;
his creativity to something he physically could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:39.040
that I think is a really good lesson&nbsp;
for everyone it's like if you can't find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.040
the time to do it the way you think you should&nbsp;
like I thought I had to have an eight-hour free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.400
day with a lot of iced tea and music playing&nbsp;
and I found that it was better to write little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.920
tiny slices even just a sentence or a paragraph&nbsp;
if you keep doing a sentence or a paragraph you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.600
will eventually get whatever it is you need&nbsp;
if you wait till you have the whole day free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:09.520
you may never so that that sort of is what I think&nbsp;
you have to do in the face of that inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.400
conflict and that conflict even happens&nbsp;
when you're a professional writer I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:21.120
run into the problem of you know I'm working&nbsp;
on a project a show or a movie or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.760
I had a project I was dying to do and sometimes&nbsp;
I would have to like take a break from my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:35.360
writing job so my job was writing but I had to&nbsp;
stop to do a little indie film that I wanted to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.440
and so I didn't take work for as long as I could&nbsp;
afford and then I would go back to work everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:50.640
has this problem the important thing is to really&nbsp;
pay attention to what is productive for you if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.000
that's writing a little bit at a time if that's&nbsp;
writing for short bursts a lot whatever works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.840
I just wasn't sure if however painful&nbsp;
or maybe not so painful some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.480
temp jobs were I know some tempting can be&nbsp;
great and some can be probably excruciating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:13.040
did that fuel you the painful moments to keep&nbsp;
going and also too you're around people that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:19.760
is their life they're like lifers at this company&nbsp;
and did you say I don't want to be that guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:28.160
yeah yeah well it's sort of I mean to a&nbsp;
certain extent this may it sounds horrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:34.560
I generally look around and say I don't want to&nbsp;
be that guy just going to the store yeah but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.400
it did do was inform me about all the parts of&nbsp;
life that I didn't know to find out what it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.600
like to work in this type of place and that&nbsp;
type of I worked in a lot of different places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.280
and in fact the first novel I wrote was about a&nbsp;
wannabe screenwriter who was an office temp so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:57.760
I guarantee you nothing nothing gets lost on a an&nbsp;
artist who's making his way in the world you got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.920
to be absorbing all of it and writing it down&nbsp;
how did you take control of your creative fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:12.160
I think recognizing that the only thing an artist&nbsp;
truly controls is the work itself and that is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:19.840
an original thought to me many far better artists&nbsp;
have said it you can't control whether your work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:26.320
sells whether it is popular even people who have&nbsp;
sold and had hits will find themselves doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:34.000
something and it flops that's just predictable&nbsp;
that that will happen so if if they can't control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.520
it if they can't guarantee a hit if they can't&nbsp;
guarantee that people like it how should we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:46.800
there's no way for anyone to know how things are&nbsp;
going to work out creatively and the way that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:53.520
become a happy artist and a productive artist&nbsp;
is to give up worrying about it you say I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:00.400
gambling I am rolling the dice on this thing&nbsp;
because I like it because I think it's fun because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:07.040
I think other people will like it I think that's&nbsp;
a big part of it just saying I am completing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:13.520
because I want it complete it has nothing to do&nbsp;
with whether or not someone else will like it you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:20.160
should at the same time adjust your expectations&nbsp;
if you are writing about a very obscure topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:27.840
or in an obscure genre then don't expect a big&nbsp;
budget plan one of the things I had to learn was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.680
if I want to write something weird and offbeat&nbsp;
write it so that I can do it myself with a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:39.280
camera and a couple actors that I get together&nbsp;
learn how to do things diy learn how to you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.520
work within the limits that you can control&nbsp;
and then you're controlling your creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.280
fight and then if somebody likes it great if&nbsp;
somebody doesn't like it at least you did it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.440
and you're more likely to do the next&nbsp;
thing better from having completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:02.480
work and put it out there and seeing the response&nbsp;
there is nothing like it for an artist to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.560
people respond to your work and it's one&nbsp;
of the things that screenwriters get to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:14.320
very infrequently most working screenwriters most&nbsp;
of their jobs they don't get out to the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:20.960
a lot of people do drafts of things that don't get&nbsp;
made now you you do that less because most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.080
are working on shows when you work on a show they&nbsp;
don't hire you if they're not going to make it but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:33.360
the experience of doing your work and putting&nbsp;
it in front of an audience starting the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.880
finishing it and presenting it is the most&nbsp;
important education you can get there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:45.680
nothing else that that replaces it so the more you&nbsp;
can do that without asking someone's permission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.640
without waiting until someone says well this will&nbsp;
make me a lot of money so I will invest in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.440
because people don't people don't put money into&nbsp;
artworks because they like them mostly they put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.480
money into artworks because they think it'll make&nbsp;
them more money so if you do something that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:09.760
good but not profitable you won't get to make it&nbsp;
it's better to do something that's good and can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.800
made really really cheaply and then you can make&nbsp;
it nobody can stop you and if you don't feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.280
directing it yourself somebody who does know&nbsp;
how to work low budget will be able to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.320
so completing projects is very important&nbsp;
to you is that completing the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:31.680
the artistic process is the best&nbsp;
education you can get if you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:38.960
write 10 scripts and finish them and ideally&nbsp;
at least get a reading of them but but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:44.560
really the best thing to do is to shoot them find&nbsp;
a way to get someone to shoot your script make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:53.360
shorts whatever that will teach you more than any&nbsp;
other education about screenwriting and if they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:10:03.840
bad so what you know do it in such a way that if&nbsp;
you lose your money it's not the end of the world.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.295
How does an artist take&nbsp;
control of their creative&nbsp;fate?

